.TH UDB80 1 "November 2026" "um80 0.4.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
udb80 \- source-level debugger for Z80 and 8080 programs in cpmemu
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B udb80
[\fIoptions\fR]
\fB\-\-run\fR \fI'cpmemu prog.com'\fR
.I prog.dbg
.br
.B udb80
[\fIoptions\fR]
\fB\-\-connect\fR \fIHOST\fR:\fIPORT\fR
.I prog.dbg
.br
.B udb80 addr2line
[\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-\-asm\fR] [\fB\-\-outer\fR] [\fB\-\-program\fR \fIFILE\fR]
.I prog.dbg ADDRESS
\&...
.br
.B udb80 sizes
.I prog.dbg
.br
.B udb80 bt\-dump
[\fB\-\-ix\fR \fIIX\fR] [\fB\-n\fR \fICOUNT\fR] [\fB\-\-program\fR \fIFILE\fR]
.I prog.dbg memory.bin SP PC
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B udb80
debugs a program that runs in
.BR cpmemu .
udb80 shows source lines, sets breakpoints on source lines, steps by
line, shows the chain of calls and prints variables.
udb80 speaks the GDB remote protocol to the stub of
.BR "cpmemu \-\-gdb" .
The program keeps all of its 64K.
udb80 keeps the symbols and the source files on the host.
.PP
udb80 reads
.IR prog.dbg ,
the file that
.B "ul80 \-\-debug\-info"
writes.
A source line comes from the
.B ;#
markers that a compiler writes with
.B \-g
.RB ( uc80 ,
.BR uplm80 ).
A program with no markers has the lines of its
.I .mac
files.
.PP
At the start, udb80 compares
.I prog.dbg
with the program.
.I prog.dbg
holds the length and the SHA-256 of the program file.
udb80 refuses a
.I prog.dbg
that does not agree with the
.I .com
file beside it, or with guest memory at 0100H.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-run " CMD"
Start cpmemu with the command
.IR CMD ,
for example
.BR "\-\-run 'cpmemu prog.com'" .
udb80 adds
.B \-\-gdb=0
and
.B \-\-gdb\-port\-file
to the command, and reads the port that cpmemu chooses.
.TP
.BI \-\-connect " HOST:PORT"
Connect to a stub that runs already, for example
.B "cpmemu \-\-gdb prog.com"
in a second terminal.
.TP
.BI \-ex " CMD"
Run the command
.IR CMD .
You can give the option more than once.
.TP
.BR \-x " \fIFILE\fR, " \-\-command " \fIFILE\fR"
Run the commands in
.IR FILE .
A line that starts with
.B #
is a comment.
.TP
.B \-\-batch
Run the commands, end the program and quit.
Without this option, udb80 reads more commands from the keyboard.
.TP
.B \-\-wait\-program
For
.BR romwbw_emu ,
where CP/M and not the stub starts the program.
udb80 tells the stub to stop when the program image of
.I prog.dbg
is at 0100H in bank 8EH.
Then udb80 starts the session.
.TP
.BI \-\-program " FILE"
The program file for the image check.
The default is the file that
.I prog.dbg
names.
.TP
.BR \-d " \fIDIR\fR, " \-\-directory " \fIDIR\fR"
A directory that holds source files.
You can give the option more than once.
.TP
.B \-\-asm
Start at the assembler level.
.TP
.B \-\-8080
Disassemble as 8080.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the usage and stop.
.SH LOCATIONS
A location is
.IR FILE : LINE ,
a routine name, a label, or
.BI * ADDRESS .
.I FILE
can be the base name of the file.
A line can have more than one address.
.B break
then sets a breakpoint at each address.
When a line has no code,
.B break
uses the next line that has code, and says so.
.SH COMMANDS
You can use a short form of a command name when only one command
starts with the short form
.RB ( b ", " c ", " s ", " n ", " bt ", " p ", " l ).
.TP
.BI break " LOCATION\fR, " tbreak " LOCATION"
Set a breakpoint.
A breakpoint from
.B tbreak
goes away after the first stop.
.TP
.BR delete " [\fIN\fR...], " "info breakpoints"
Delete breakpoints, or list them.
.TP
.B continue
Run until a breakpoint, the end of the program, or ^C.
.TP
.BR step " [\fIN\fR], " next " [\fIN\fR], " stepi " [\fIN\fR]"
Run to the next source line.
.B step
goes into a call.
.B next
runs over a call.
.B stepi
runs one instruction.
.TP
.B finish
Run until the current routine returns.
.TP
.BR backtrace " [" shadow | frame "] [\fIN\fR]"
Show the frames
.RB ( bt ).
See
.BR BACKTRACE .
.TP
.BR list " [\fILOCATION\fR], " list " \fIFIRST\fR,\fILAST\fR"
Show source lines.
.TP
.BR disassemble " [\fISTART\fR,\fIEND\fR | \fISTART\fR,+\fILENGTH\fR | \fILOCATION\fR]"
Show instructions.
The default is the routine that holds the current address.
.TP
.BR "info registers" " [\fINAME\fR...]"
Show the registers and the flags.
.TP
.BR "info symbols" " [\fIPATTERN\fR | \fIADDRESS\fR], " "info line" " [\fILOCATION\fR]"
List the labels, name the label of an address, or show the address range
of a line.
.TP
.BI x/ NFU " ADDRESS"
Show memory.
.I N
is a count.
.I F
is
.BR x ", " d ", " u ", " c ", " s
or
.BR i .
.I U
is
.B b
(1 byte),
.B h
(2 bytes) or
.B w
(4 bytes).
.TP
.BI print [/ F ] " EXPRESSION"
Show a value: a number, a register
.RB ( $pc ", " $sp ", " $hl ", " $a ),
a label, a variable,
.BR + ", " \- ", " &
and
.BR * .
.BR print/x
shows hex.
.TP
.BR "info locals" ", " "info args" ", " frame " [\fIN\fR], " up " [\fIN\fR], " down " [\fIN\fR], " ptype ", " whatis
Show the variables of the selected frame, select a frame, or show a type.
A variable that the optimizer keeps in a register can show
.BR <not available> .
Compile the routine with
.B \-\-optnone
to keep each variable in its slot.
.TP
.B sizes
Show the bytes of each routine.
.TP
.BI monitor " COMMAND"
Send a command to the stub, for example
.BR "monitor bt" .
.TP
.BR "set level" " auto|src|asm|outer|both, " "set cpu" " z80|8080, " "set shadow" " on|off"
Choose the level of the lines, the CPU of the disassembler, and the
source of the backtrace.
.TP
.BR kill ", " quit ", " echo " \fITEXT\fR, " help
.SH BACKTRACE
.B backtrace
has two sources.
The first is the shadow call stack of the stub
.RB ( "monitor bt" ).
The second is the frame records of
.IR prog.dbg ,
which need only the registers and the memory.
udb80 uses the shadow call stack when the stub has one.
Each frame has a tag:
.TP
.B [shadow]
The frame comes from the shadow call stack.
.TP
.B [frame]
The frame comes from the frame records.
.TP
.B [guess]
The records did not give the return address.
udb80 found a word on the stack that follows a CALL instruction.
The frame can be wrong.
.SH OTHER FORMS
.TP
.B addr2line
Show the routine and the line of each address.
An address is
.BR 0x0108 ,
.B 0108H
or a decimal number.
With
.BR \-r ,
each address is a return address, and udb80 looks up the byte before it.
Use
.B \-r
for the addresses of
.BR "cpmemu \-\-backtrace" .
.B \-\-asm
shows the assembler line.
.B \-\-outer
shows the outer level (for example the C line under a BASIC line).
.TP
.B sizes
Show the bytes of each routine, the biggest first, and the total.
Use the list to choose the routines for
.BR "uc80 \-\-optnone" .
.TP
.B bt\-dump
Show the backtrace of a program that has stopped, from the frame records,
a memory image and the values of SP and PC.
Make the image with
.BR "cpmemu \-\-save\-memory=memory.bin prog.com" .
The image has 65536 bytes, from address 0.
Give
.B \-\-ix
when PC is in the IX range of a routine.
.B \-n
shows only
.I COUNT
frames.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
With
.BR \-\-batch ,
a command gave an error.
For
.BR addr2line ,
an address has no line.
.TP
.B 2
udb80 could not start: for example,
.I prog.dbg
does not agree with the program, or the connection failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Build a C program for the debugger:
.PP
.nf
.RS
LIB=$(uc80 \-\-print\-lib\-dir)
uc80 \-g \-o prog.mac prog.c
um80 \-\-debug\-info \-o prog.rel prog.mac
ul80 \-\-debug\-info \-o prog.com prog.rel $LIB/libc.lib $LIB/runtime.lib
.RE
.fi
.PP
Start cpmemu, stop in a function, and show the frames:
.PP
.nf
.RS
udb80 prog.dbg \-\-run 'cpmemu prog.com'
(udb80) break sum
(udb80) continue
(udb80) bt
(udb80) print n
.RE
.fi
.PP
Run the same commands with no keyboard:
.PP
.nf
.RS
udb80 prog.dbg \-\-run 'cpmemu prog.com' \-\-batch \e
    \-ex 'break prog.c:4' \-ex continue \-ex bt
.RE
.fi
.PP
Name the line of each return address that
.B "cpmemu \-\-backtrace"
printed:
.PP
.nf
.RS
udb80 addr2line \-r prog.dbg 0x0125 0x012e
.RE
.fi
.SH REQUIREMENTS
udb80 is in um80 0.4.0 and later
.RB ( "pip install \(dqum80>=0.4.0\(dq" ).
The emulator must have
.BR \-\-gdb :
cpmemu 4.11.0 and later, or romwbw_emu 1.50 and later with
.BR \-\-wait\-program .
udb80 does not need GDB.
.SH LIMITS
.IP \(bu 2
udb80 runs one program on one stub.
.IP \(bu 2
No
.BR watch ,
conditions,
.BR call ,
or
.B set
of memory.
.IP \(bu 2
No cast, assignment, comparison,
.B sizeof
or function call in an expression.
A float is not converted.
.IP \(bu 2
With
.B \-\-run
and no
.BR \-\-batch ,
the program and udb80 both read the terminal.
For a program that reads the keyboard, start cpmemu in a second terminal
and use
.BR \-\-connect .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I prog.dbg
The linked debug file from
.BR "ul80 \-\-debug\-info" .
.TP
.I prog.com
The program.
udb80 checks it against
.IR prog.dbg .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dbg2elf (1),
.BR ul80 (1),
.BR um80 (1),
.BR gdb (1)
.PP
docs/udb80.md, docs/debug_info.md and docs/frame_records.md of
um80_and_friends: https://github.com/avwohl/um80_and_friends
.PP
docs/gdb_stub.md of cpmemu: https://github.com/avwohl/cpmemu
